NAME
    tree — Display the contents of a directory as a tree.

SYNOPSIS
    tree [path] [depth]

DESCRIPTION
    The 'tree' command displays files and directories in a tree-like structure.
    Directories are shown with a / at the end of their name.
    By default, the tree starts from the current workspace and displays up to
    3 levels deep.

OPTIONS
    path    The directory to start from.
    Defaults to the current workspace.

    depth   Maximum number of directory levels to display.
            Defaults to 3.

EXAMPLES
    > tree
        Displays the workspace directory and its contents up to 3 levels deep.

    > tree projects
        Displays the contents of the projects directory.

    > tree . 5
        Displays the current directory up to 5 levels deep.

    > tree projects 2
        Displays the projects directory up to 2 levels deep.

FUN FACTS
    - Directories always end with a / so you can spot them easily.
    - Folders are displayed before files.
    - Everything is sorted alphabetically.
    - ├── means there are more entries after this one.
    - └── means this is the last entry at this level.
    - │   shows that the tree continues further down.
    - tree is basically ls that decided to grow branches.

